    "passageText": "Noise maps are used by planners to estimate how much sound residents experience from roads, railways and aircraft. Rather than placing a microphone outside every home, researchers combine traffic volumes, vehicle speeds, building shapes and other information in computer models that estimate sound levels across large areas. These maps are useful for identifying broad patterns, but they are not perfect descriptions of individual experience. A model may calculate average sound over a day or night, while a resident may be most disturbed by a small number of sudden events. Two streets with similar average levels can therefore feel very different if one has regular background traffic and the other has occasional extremely loud vehicles. Buildings also alter exposure. A flat facing a busy road may be noisy at the front but relatively quiet in rooms facing a courtyard. Open windows, insulation and floor level can all change what is heard indoors. Noise maps generally cannot capture every one of these details. Despite these limitations, mapping can guide decisions about barriers, road surfaces, speed limits and building design. It can also help authorities identify neighbourhoods where further measurement is needed. The maps are most useful when treated as a screening tool rather than as a perfect record of what every resident hears. The challenge is therefore similar to many forms of environmental modelling: simplifying reality enough to make patterns visible without forgetting that the simplification has limits.",
